1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Cloud Code hands you a set of session tools and a slash command menu,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and using them well is what keeps a long day of work from turning into a mess.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
We'll walk through reopening past sessions, keeping context clean,

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and the difference between built-in commands and the ones your team writes themselves. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's lock down three terms this whole lesson leans on.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, a session. That's a single conversation tied to the project directory you launched it in,

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and Cloud Code saves it to disk as you go, so you can come back to it later.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Second, a slash command. That's a shortcut you type with a leading slash to run an action.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Anything from clearing your context to kicking off a workflow your team wrote.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, compaction. When your context window fills up, compaction replaces the older history

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
with a shorter summary, freeing space while keeping your recent thread alive.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Session slash command compaction. Those three carry the rest of the lesson, so keep them handy.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Here's something worth internalizing early. A session isn't just the text scrolling on

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
your screen. It's a real record written to disk, and that's what lets you reopen it.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A few facts follow from that. Each session is tied to the project directory

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
you started it in, so it comes back in context, not floating loose.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The transcript is written continuously as you work, which means closing your terminal,

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
or even a crash, doesn't lose your conversation. And old sessions get cleaned up after a retention

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
window you control through settings, 30 days by default. One caution, treat that raw transcript

20
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
as internal. Don't parse it by hand. Reopen your work through the commands instead.

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
So how do you actually get a past session back? Three moves cover almost everything.

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
The first is the continue flag. Launch with it, and Claude code reopens the most recent session

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
in your current directory, dropping you right back where you left off. No picking required.

24
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
The second is the resume flag. That opens a picker listing your past sessions,

25
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
or if you already know which one you want, you name it and jump straight there. The third is

26
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
resuming from inside a live session, which lets you switch over to a different past conversation

27
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
without quitting first. One thing to know, this lookup is scoped to your current project directory

28
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
and its work trees, so run it from the right place. Now let's separate two commands. People

29
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
constantly mix up, compact and clear. Compacting is the gentle one. When your window fills,

30
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,000
it replaces the older turns with a summary, but it keeps your recent decisions and your current task

31
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
alive in condensed form, so you just keep working through it. Clearing is the hard reset. It empties

32
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
the window and starts a new conversation with a blank slate, so nothing from before carries into

33
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
what comes next. Here's the part that trips people up. Clearing doesn't delete your conversation.

34
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The previous history is still saved on disk and still resumable later. You're emptying the live

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
context, not throwing the record away. Let's clear up a common wrong assumption. Clod code doesn't

36
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:26,000
remember your last session on its own. Every fresh session starts blank, seeing none of yesterday's

37
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
conversation by default. That sounds limiting, but it's the whole design, and you have three

38
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
deliberate ways to carry continuity forward. Your clod.md reloads your standing rules and

39
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
conventions at every single launch, so the durable stuff is always there. Resume pulls a specific

40
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
past session back into view when you actually need that history, and auto memory restores the

41
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
learnings clod saved for itself across sessions. So continuity is something you set up on purpose

42
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
through config and deliberate resuming, not something the tool hands you for free.

43
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Built-in commands are the ones that ship with the CLI, and it helps to think about them in four groups

44
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:17,000
rather than memorizing a long list. The first group is session and context, the commands that reopen,

45
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:25,000
reset, or summarize a conversation, like resume, clear, and compact. The second is model and config,

46
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
which let you switch the model, the effort level, or a setting without leaving your session. The

47
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
third is setup and memory, things that scaffold a repo or manage your instructions, like init and

48
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,000
memory. And the fourth is quality and workflow, bundled skills that run a whole procedure for you,

49
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
from code review to planning. The exact names shift over time, so learn the groups, not the roster.

50
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Custom commands are the ones your team writes, and these are three facts you already know from

51
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
the components lesson, so let's just tap them quickly. The file name sets the name you type

52
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
after the slash. Location sets the scope, project folder for the team through version control,

53
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
home folder for you alone. Picture a review checklist your team runs before every merge.

54
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Save it as review.md under .clod, commands in the repo, and the moment a teammate pulls,

55
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
they can type slash review and get the exact same procedure. And custom commands and skills are one

56
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,000
system now, so a single file gives you a reusable, shareable action with no registration step to wire

57
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
it up. Let's tie the whole lesson into one habit, a session hygiene loop you run deliberately

58
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
instead of cleaning up once and forgetting it. Start by pruning stale tool output, the giant

59
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
file or search result that's already done its job and is just costing you tokens. Compact when the

60
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
window fills, but the thread still matters, so you shrink the history without losing your place.

61
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Clear or start fresh when the context has turned into pure baggage and a blank slate is faster,

62
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and resume a saved session when you genuinely need that earlier history back.

63
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Run that loop on purpose and a long session stays as sharp at the end as it was at the start.

64
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Every session saves to disk and reopens with the continue flag or resume. The compact command

65
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:31,000
summarizes, clear empties the context entirely, and neither one deletes your history. Continuity

66
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:38,000
between sessions comes from clod.md, auto memory, and deliberate resuming, never for free. And built

67
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
in commands ship with the CLI, while custom ones are files your team writes and shares with each

68
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
other. That wraps up managing sessions and slash commands. Nice work getting through that,

69
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
I'll see you in the next one.

